# loop-count.bot -- a loop whose body does integer work on bindings local to
# the iteration. The loop is driven by an outer recursion, since the
# language has no mutation.
#
# Canonical surface-Botlish source (CANONICAL-SURFACE-BENCHMARKS.md);
# migrated from the historical raw-core-IR fixture bench/loop-count.ir,
# which remains in bench/ only as a frozen input for historical audit-
# reproduction tooling (see that report) -- it is no longer canonical
# benchmark source.
#
# work's loop body recomputes a = i*3, b = a+7 every iteration and exits
# with b - a the first time b >= 0. Since drive only ever calls work(i) for
# i in [1, 500], b is always >= 10 there, so the loop's condition is always
# true on its very first iteration -- work never actually continues to a
# second one for any input this benchmark exercises (confirmed: HIR range
# analysis proves this exact fact independently, see hir-call-facts.test's
# call-facts-work-1 / M6-RANGE-DECIDED-BRANCH-LOWERING.md). That means a
# bare `loop:` immediately followed by `return` on its only reachable
# iteration is *exactly* equivalent to the old `break VALUE`, which is no
# longer expressible in surface Botlish (PAYLOAD-FREE-BREAK.md): the same
# migration 08-return.bot already established for an identical shape
# ("the original loop never actually iterated more than once ... break b
# and return b were already observationally identical there"). This is not
# a workaround invented to dodge the missing break payload -- it is the
# same construct a Botlish programmer would reach for today to express
# "compute this loop-local value, then leave with it".
#
# expect: 3500

fn work(i):
    loop:
        a = i * 3
        b = a + 7
        if b >= 0:
            return b - a
        else:
            continue

fn drive(i, total):
    if i <= 0:
        return total
    drive(i - 1, total + work(i))

drive(500, 0)
